          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could find does not name a priority group for influenza vaccination. It does not describe a list of high-risk people who should go first.\n\nWhat it does say comes from the influenza guidance (Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment). United States guidance recommends yearly flu vaccination for everyone aged 6 months and older who has no reason not to have it. It describes this as routine, not as something limited to high-risk groups. It also says the usual aim in the northern hemisphere is to vaccinate before the end of October, and that vaccinating later is still worthwhile while the virus is circulating.\n\nA separate heart guideline (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis) lists annual flu vaccination as one of the measures for people with coronary artery disease. Those results did not say whether that group is a priority.\n\nMy search results did not cover which groups, if any, are given priority when vaccine supply is limited. If that is what you need, a doctor, pharmacist, or your national or local public health agency would be able to tell you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
